.c` reads the graph, `w` and\n196:- `mc_vsmemo_test.py`: 1,500 random graphs to 13 vertices, at `pw - 2 .. pw`,\n198:- `mc_checkF_test.py`: 365 genuine certificates verify. Each with one set\n252:gcc -O2 -march=native -o vsmemo vsmemo.c\n253:gcc -O2 -o checkF checkF.c\n257:python mc_vsmemo_test.py                  # vsmemo against brute force\n258:python mc_checkF_test.py                  # checkF accepts genuine, rejects damaged certificates\n259:python mc_build.py                        # replay every minor, compare with the 2026-10-11 record\n273:xz -dk proofs/myciel7_m110_w70.F.xz && ./checkF myciel7_m110_edges.txt 70 proofs/myciel7_m110_w70.F\n276:./vsmemo myciel7_m110_edges.txt 71 -o\n285:./vsmemo myciel7_full_edges.txt 72 -o     # a width-72 layout\n  commands below regenerate them byte for byte, and the SHA-256 of each raw file\n  is recorded in `results.log`.\n\n## myciel7: 71 or 72\n\n**Unsettled.** `71 <= pw(myciel7) <= 72`.\n\n- **Lower bound 72.** By contraction, minors of 115–125 vertices all have a\n  width-71 layout. Up to 120 vertices this was confirmed here: the rule-free\n  search finds a width-71 layout of the 120-vertex min-d minor at once. So a\n  certificate of 72 needs a minor of at least about 126 vertices.\n\n  On the 130-vertex minors (both rules) and the 140-vertex min-d minor, the\n  rule-free search exhausted a cap of 250 million stored sets (about 20 GB)\n  without an answer. The rule-based search of 2026-10-11 also stopped\n  unfinished there, at 50–237 million nodes in 300 s.\n\n  The proved forced-vertex rule (`-f`, below) did not help. On the\n  110-vertex minor it visits the same 3,398,378 sets. On the 130-vertex min-d\n  minor it also stopped at 250 million sets, after 1,113 s.\n- **Upper bound 71.** Four 900-second simulated-annealing runs on the full\n  graph (`vsanneal.c`) reached 73, 74, 72 and 72; the last of these started from the width-72\n  layout. Two further 1,800-second runs from that layout stayed at 72. In 20–30 s the same annealer reaches the\n  optimum on myciel6 (38) and on the 70-vertex corpus minor (20). A depth-first search for a width-71 layout of the\n  full graph, ordered by boundary, gave no answer after 100 million sets. The\n  same search finds a width-72 layout in 211 nodes\n  (`myciel7_w72_order.txt`, checked by `mc_layout_check.py`).\n\nSo the evidence, such as it is, leans towards 72. A certificate of 72 would\nneed either a smarter minor than the two greedy rules produce, or a\nrefutation on 130+ vertices with more memory or a sound pruning rule. The\nforced-vertex rule below is the only one used here, and it is proved.\n\n## Method\n\n### (i) Replaying the minors\n\n`mc_graph.py` uses no networkx and imports nothing from the project. It\nprovides:\n\n- readers for `.dgf` and for both MOSP file formats, building the MOSP graph:\n  customers as vertices, a clique per pattern;\n- the two greedy contraction rules, rewritten from their description. The\n  minimum-degree vertex, ties by label, is contracted into a neighbour: min-d\n  takes the neighbour of least degree, least-c the neighbour with fewest common\n  neighbours;\n- `replay`, which applies a sequence `[(keep, absorbed), ...]` and asserts that\n  each pair is an edge of the *current* graph. It merges the two endpoints,\n  unions their neighbourhoods and drops the loop. It then rebuilds the quotient\n  graph independently from the resulting branch sets and asserts it equals the\n  replayed graph.\n\nFor each claim, `mc_build.py` regenerates the sequence, replays it, and replays\na second sequence derived from the branch sets alone (a BFS tree inside each\nbranch set), so connectivity of every branch set is checked as well. It then\ncompares the minor with the 2026-10-11 record and writes `<claim>.json`:\nsequence, branch sets, minor edges and hash.\n\n`mc_crosscheck_readers.py` is the only file that imports project code. It checks\nthat the readers here produce the same labelled graphs as\n`pathwidth_solver/bench/readers.read_any` and\n`customer_inter.customer_graph.build_customer_graph`. They do, for all six\ninput graphs.\n\nTwo claims had no minor recorded on 2026-10-11, `6-1_0` at 120 vertices and\n`SP4` at 96. For them, the minor certified here is the one the same rule\nproduces.\n\n### (ii) The rule-free refutation and its certificate\n\n`vsmemo.c` decides `pathwidth(H) <= w` by depth-first search over layout\n10:## What this document is about\n81:## 0. Summary\n87:## 1. The baseline ledger and the frozen targets (item 01)\n103:### 1.1 What was done\n165:### 1.2 Findings\n241:### 1.3 Kill verdict and what this does not claim\n257:### 1.4 Tables (generated; `tables.md`)\n472:## 2. Family E by search: the ceiling on every target, the exact profile at `n ≤ 22`, the floor by branch and bound (item 02)\n497:### 2.1 What was done\n561:### 2.2 Numbers\n660:### 2.3 Through the harness\n686:### 2.4 Kill verdict and compute\n703:### 2.5 What this does not claim\n717:## 3. Family E exact at full depth: the inner minimum by SAT with DRAT; the gap bracket closed where it settles (item 03)\n734:### 3.1 What was done\n807:### 3.2 Numbers\n924:### 3.3 Through the harness\n939:### 3.4 Kill verdict and compute\n966:### 3.5 What this does not claim\nlevels, 2 × 10⁶ conflicts, 120 s per call) **and fully in reach to 60**,\nwhere every bracket closed in seconds. The family's floor downstream is the\nexact floor where a verified certificate proves it (144 instances,\n`instances.csv::floor_after`) and the cap-12 floor elsewhere; nothing in\nthe floor rests on the search's quality or on a censored level. The second\nkill condition — an UNSAT at a `(t, b)` where item 02's enumeration has a\nset — occurred on **0** calls (the self-test at `n ≤ 12` agrees with the\nenumeration on 3,000+ `(t, b)` pairs over 100 graphs, and the run's SAT\nanswers all have `|N[set]| ≤ b` re-evaluated from the masks).\n\n**Compute.** Instance time 5.76 core-hours (solve 5.24, in-run drat-trim\n0.45, the rest the forks and the writes), the checker sweep 0.31, the\nharness run under 0.01 (a lookup), the smoke run and four timing probes\n≈ 0.1: **≈ 6.2 core-hours** against the item's 6; the overrun is the\nindependent re-check the acceptance criteria require. 8 workers at\n`nice 19`; the machine's one-minute load was 2–8 during the run (the\nowner's `run_unlimited.py` was not running). Wall 45 min.\n\n### 3.5 What this does not claim\n\nThat the SAT route scales beyond the boundary it measures (a call censored\nby its conflict budget or its deadline says nothing about `f(t)`, and no\ncensored call contributes to any floor); that a larger budget would not\nmove the boundary (not measured; the conflict counts of the finished calls\nat 75–100 say a 10× budget would at most reach one more band); that the\nexact floor on the 144 reached instances is the family's best (levels the\nceiling did not mark, and the three unreached instances, were not asked;\nat most 8 levels per instance and 240 s, which at `n ≥ 75` meant one or\ntwo levels); that the ceiling after the run is the family's value (it is an\nupper estimate still, exact only where it meets the floor: 72 targets); any\nsolver change (no candidate reaches `_lower_bound`; the family's bound as a\nbound inside the search was a net loss, `reports/expansion_bound.md`, which\nthis item does not revisit). The certificate for an instance is a proof\nabout *that instance file*: the checker reads the `.mosp` file the run\nwrote beside it and ties it to the benchmark source by the rows' hash\nrecorded in the certificate, not by re-reading the benchmark file's own\nformat. \"We found no prior report\" of DRAT-certified vertex-isoperimetric\nprofiles on MOSP instances; the bound is Harper (1966), the totalizer\nBailleux & Boufkhad (2003), the checker drat-trim (Wetzler, Heule & Hunt\n2014).","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9f928aeb-dd8b-4eb2-ae60-0ddf3b071136","uuid":"f70cbb72-6872-45fe-b275-e43ce503955e",