t 2 (session it01_fix2, this one). Cause found and fixed.** `paper1/false_refutation_hunt.py::run` installs SIGTERM and SIGINT handlers (`on_term`, which only sets a flag) and never restores them. `tests/test_false_refutation_hunt.py::test_stop_and_resume` calls `run` twice, so every test after it — 774 of them, measured with a plugin that records `signal.getsignal(SIGTERM)` after each test — runs in a process with a Python-level SIGTERM handler, and every `fork`-started `multiprocessing.Pool` worker inherits it. `Pool.terminate()` ends workers with SIGTERM (its `_help_stuff_finish` takes the task queue's reader lock and keeps it, so the `None` sentinel cannot reach a worker still waiting for that lock); a worker with the inherited handler absorbs the signal, returns to its semaphore wait, and `p.join()` blocks forever. Intermittent because the worker only needs the SIGTERM when it is the one still waiting on the reader lock at exit (or deadlocked after a fork from a threaded parent, the hazard attempt 1 removed); the handler turns that race into a hang. Evidence: the orphaned worker PID 2442642 that attempt 1's diagnostic run left behind (forked 23:14:34, `cmd` = pytest, one thread, asleep on a futex) had SIGTERM in `SigCgt`, survived `kill` (SIGTERM) and died on SIGKILL. The fix: `run` records the previous handlers and restores them in a `finally`; `test_stop_and_resume` asserts the handlers are what they were. After the fix the plugin records no test with a non-default SIGTERM handler. Also killed the leftover orphan.  Seen once and not addressed: in attempt 1's run of 23:14:59, under its own parallel pytest runs plus the owner's load, `test_stop_and_resume` failed with `KeyError: 'tested'` — the spawned hunt worker had not reported stats within the test's 8 s deadline, so the checkpoint's counters held only the base keys. A load-dependent flake of the test's budget, recorded here.  **Gate after fix 2: PASS** (`python3 Ralph_Loops/loop0013/gate.py`, 2026-11-03 00